Two defects in one function. First, the replacements are applied to the *result* of the previous replacement, so `&amp;lt;script&amp;gt;` is decoded twice into `<script>` — content that was escaped stays escaped no longer. Second, `<` survives tag stripping precisely because it arrived entity-encoded, and only then becomes a literal `<`. The result is provider/page text that can re-introduce markup into the model's context after the sanitiser ran. Impact is low (the text is not re-parsed as HTML by this server, and the model treats it as text, which is the `L4-4` problem), but the sanitiser must not be order-dependent in this way: today `&amp;lt;` and a literal `<` take different paths, and only one of them is stripped.

