There is no `ChannelOptions.maxConnections`, no read/idle timeout (`IdleStateHandler` or `readTimeout`), and no keep-alive bound; `respond` always sets `Connection: close` (which limits one request per connection) but does nothing about a peer that opens connections and never finishes a request. Each connection costs a channel plus handler, and each completed request hops to a `Task`. Since the supported exposed shape is a reverse proxy in front, the proxy normally absorbs this — but the code itself claims to be "a minimal, correct HTTP/1.1 server", and `--host 0.0.0.0` is a supported (warned) opt-in. Slowloris-style exhaustion of the file-descriptor/memory budget is therefore available to anyone who can reach the port. No auth is involved (recorded decision); this finding is only about connection-resource bounds.

```swift
// Logging.swift:34-47
public static let standardErrorSink: @Sendable (String) -> Void = { line in
    var text = line
    text.append("\n")
    let bytes = Array(text.utf8)
    bytes.withUnsafeBufferPointer { buffer in
        guard let base = buffer.baseAddress else { return }
        var offset = 0
        while offset < buffer.count {
            let written = write(2, base + offset, buffer.count - offset)
            if written <= 0 { break }
            offset += written
        }
    }
}
```

`Log.emit` calls `sink(line)` synchronously, and `sink` loops on a raw `write` with no `O_NONBLOCK` handling and no retry on `EINTR` (a `-1` simply breaks, silently truncating the line). If stderr is a pipe whose reader is slow or stopped, the write blocks once the pipe buffer fills, and because `emit` is called from actors and task bodies (the orchestrator logs every search; `JinaReaderFetcher` logs every fetch), that stall is propagated into the search pipeline — one search = one log line, so sustained traffic against a stopped stderr consumer will stall tool calls rather than fail them. The `EINTR` break also means a signal can cut a diagnostic in half. Preconditions: stderr piped (common for stdio MCP hosts) with a non-draining or stopped reader.

